course: dictionary
lesson: 14917
title: A word from old dining halls
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A refectory is a large dining hall, the room where many people eat together. The word is old and a little formal. «The monks ate their soup in the refectory in silence.» The clues are monks, soup and eating together, so a refectory must be a place for meals.
explain: Some schools and colleges still have a refectory, but most now call it the dining hall, the canteen or the cafeteria. «The school refectory was full of noise at lunchtime.» You can swap in dining hall and the sentence still makes sense, so you have found its meaning.
explain: A refectory table is a different use of the word. It is a long, narrow wooden table, the kind where many people sit in rows. «The refectory had six long tables.» Read the words around refectory and you can tell whether it names a room or a piece of furniture.
fact: A refectory is a large dining hall.
fact: Refectories are often found in monasteries, convents, colleges and old schools.
fact: A «refectory table» is a long, narrow wooden dining table.
fact: Today most schools say «dining hall», «canteen» or «cafeteria» instead of refectory.
fact: Context clue: if the sentence is about eating in a big room, refectory means the dining hall.
exercise: «The monks ate their soup in the refectory in silence.» What is a refectory? | *A large dining hall | A tall bell tower | A garden with a stone wall | A small bedroom for one monk
exercise: What does a «refectory table» look like? | *Long, narrow and wooden | Tiny and folding | Round and made of glass | Painted gold all over
exercise: «The refectory had six long tables, and each table had twelve seats.» How many seats were there in all? | 60 | *72 | 62 | 18
test: Which modern word do most schools use instead of refectory? | Playground | Library | *Dining hall | Gym
test: Where was a refectory most likely to be found? | *A monastery | A railway station | A swimming pool | A farm shed
exam: What does refectory mean? | *A large dining hall, especially in a monastery or old school | A kind of kitchen oven | A long wooden bench for one person | A quiet room for reading
